Article 16: Classification of offences
Offences are classified according to their gravity as follows:
1º felony;
2º misdemeanour;
3º petty offence.

Article 25: Penalties applicable to institutions and organizations of the
State or non-governmental organizations with legal personality
Penalties applicable to the institutions and organizations of the State, companies, cooperatives, non-governmental organizations with legal personality are the following:
1º a fine; 1
2º the ban on the conduct of one or several 2 professional or social activities for a fixed period of time;
3º confiscation of the object used or intended 3 for use in the commission of the offence or the proceeds thereof;
4º publication of the penalty pronounced. 4

Companies, cooperatives, non- governmental organizations with legal personality may also be punishable by the following penalties:
1° dissolution; 1
2° permanent closure of establishments in 2 which incriminated acts have been committed or which have been used to commit such acts;
3° permanent exclusion from public 3 procurement contracts, either indefinitely or for a fixed period of time provided for by relevant laws;
4° the ban on issuing a cheque, a credit card 4 or negotiable instrument;
5° placement under judicial supervision. 5

Article 42: Deprivation of civic rights
The deprivation of civic rights consists of the following:
1º removal or prohibition from any public 1 service;
2º deprivation of all or part of political rights; 2
3º deprivation of the right to wear decorations 3 of honour;
4º prohibition to act as an expert witness or a 4 witness to deeds and to testify in court, except in case of providing information;
5º loss of legal capacity to act as a family 5 council member, a guardian, a trustee, a guardian replacement, a guardian of intellectually disabled persons except in case of his/ her own children;
6º deprivation of the right to possess arms; 6

7º prohibition to use a negotiable instrument 7 or a credit card;
8º debarment from participating in public 8 procurement;
9º deprivation of the right to leave the national 9 territory;
10º deprivation of the right to perform such 1 functions as may be specified by the court.

Article 57: Reduction of a penalty in case of provocation
If the judge is satisfied with elements that constitute provocation of the offender, penalties are reduced in the following proportions:
1º in case of a felony punishable by life 1 imprisonment, the penalty is reduced to imprisonment for a term of not less than two
(2) years and not more than five (5) years;
2º in case of a felony punishable by 2 imprisonment of not less than five (5) years and not more than twenty-five (25) years, the penalty is reduced to imprisonment of not less than six (6) months and not more than two (2) years;
3º in case of a misdemeanour, the penalty is 3 reduced to imprisonment of not less than eight (8) days but less than six (6) months;
4º in case of a petty offence, no penalty is 4 imposed.

Article 59: Certain mitigating circumstances decided by a judge
The judge may reduce penalties, especially when:

1º the accused, before the commencement of 1 prosecution, pleads guilty and sincerely seek forgiveness from the victim and the
Rwandan society and expresses remorse and repairs the damage caused as much as would be expected from him/her;
2º the accused turns himself/herself in 2 to competent judicial authority before or during the pre-trial proceedings;
3º at the outset of the trial in the first 3 instance, the accused pleads guilty by a sincere confession;
4º the offence has minor consequences. 4

Article 62: Penalties in case of concurrence of offences
In case of ideal concurrence of offences referred to in item One of Paragraph 3 of
Article 61 of this Law, the judge imposes penalties provided for the most serious offence.
In case of ideal concurrence of offences referred to in item 2º of Paragraph 3 of
Article 61 of this Law, the judge imposes the maximum penalty provided for the most serious offence.
In case of real concurrence of offences, the judge imposes penalties for each offence and combine them, taking into account the following:
1º the penalty of life imprisonment outweighs 1 all other penalties of imprisonment;
2º the combination of penalties of fixed-term 2 imprisonment cannot be more than twice the maximum of the most severe penalty;
3º the combination of the sentences of 3 community service cannot exceed two (2) years;

4º the combination of penalties of fine cannot 4 be more than twice the maximum of the most severe penalty;
5º the combination of penalties of ban on 5 residence or compulsory residence in a particular location or penalties of deprivation of civic rights cannot be more than twenty-five years (25).
Any accessory penalty is imposed even if it is provided only for one of the concurrent offences.

Article 3: Conditions for application of suspension of penalty
Article 64 of Law nº 68/2018 of 30/08/2018 determining offences and penalties in general is amended as follows:
(1) « If the convicted person was not previously convicted of another penalty of imprisonment exceeding 6 months, the court may, through a reasoned decision, order suspension of the execution of all or part of the principal or accessory penalty imposed provided that the principal penalty imposed does not exceed 5 years.
(2) The court decision ordering suspension of penalty –
(a) states the grounds on which it is based;
(b) states when the suspension of the penalty of imprisonment or the penalty of community service begins and when it ends;
(c) may order suspension of all or part of the penalty of imprisonment or of fine;
(d) may order suspension of the principal or accessory penalty.
(3) The court decision ordering suspension of penalty is rendered during the trial for the offence committed. »

Article 85: Grounds for non-criminal liability
There is no criminal liability if:
1º the accused is a child under the age of 1 fourteen (14) years;
2º the accused was insane during the 2 commission of the offence;
A person who deliberately deprives himself/herself of mental faculties during the commission of an offence is criminally liable, even if such deprivation was not intended to commit an offence.

Article 86: Grounds for exemption from criminal liability
Grounds for exemption from criminal liability that are inherent to the offender are admitted if :
1º a person commits an offence under the 1 effect of a force or under duress to such an extent that he/she cannot resist;
2º a person commits an act under orders of a 2 legitimate authority, unless such an act is evidently unlawful;

3º a person commits an offence as a result of 3 an error of law or an error of fact if such an error entirely removes the intention to commit an offence;
Grounds for exemption from criminal liability that are inherent to the act are admitted if :
1º a person acts in self-defence in the 1 following cases: a) fending off a person who breaks into or a enters by force or trickery an inhabited place; b) defends himself/herself against thieves or b other wrongdoers;
2º a person commits the following acts: 2 a) an act of self-defence in case of danger; a b) an act of defending another person in b danger; c) an act committed to interrupt the c commission of an offence of damage to property in order to protect the property.
The exemption of criminal liability due to the grounds referred to under Paragraph 2 of this Article is only admitted if the means used by the offender to ensure self-defence or protect himself/herself or another person from danger or interrupt damage to the property he/she protected are proportional to the gravity of the threat.

Article 91: Definition of crime of genocide
The crime of genocide is any of the following acts committed with intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnic, racial or religious group as such, whether in time of peace or in time of war:
1º killing members of the group; 1
2º causing serious bodily or mental harm to 2 members of the group;

3º deliberately inflicting on the group 3 conditions of life calculated to bring about its physical destruction in whole or in part;
4º imposing measures intended to prevent 4 births within the group;
5º forcibly transferring children of the group 5 to another group.

Article 94: Definition of the crime against humanity
The crime against humanity is any of the following acts committed as part of a widespread or systematic attack directed against any civilian population:
1º murder; 1
2º extermination; 2
3º enslavement; 3
4º deportation or forcible transfer of 4 population;
5º imprisonment or other severe deprivation of 5 physical liberty against a person in violation of law;
6º torture; 6

7º rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution, 7 enforced sterilization, or any other form of sexual violence of comparable gravity;
8º persecution against a person on political, 8 ethnic, religious grounds or any other form of discrimination;
9º enforced disappearance of persons; 9
10º the crime of apartheid; 1
11º other inhumane acts of a similar character 1 intentionally causing great suffering or serious injury to mental or physical health.

Article 96: Definition of a war crime
A war crime is any of the following acts committed during armed conflict and directed against persons or property protected under the provisions of the
Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949 and their additional Protocols I and II of 8
June 1977 and Protocol III of 8 December
2005:
1º wilful killing; 1
2º torture or inhuman treatment, including 2 biological experiments;
3º wilfully causing great suffering or serious 3 injury to body or health;

4º extensive destruction and appropriation of 4 property, not justified by military necessity and carried out unlawfully and wantonly such as buildings dedicated to religion, charity or education, historical buildings dedicated to works of art and science;
5º compelling a prisoner of war or other 5 protected person to serve in the forces of a hostile power, in its intelligence or administrative services;
6º wilfully depriving a prisoner of war or other 6 protected person of the right to regular and fair trial;
7º forced deportation, displacement of the 7 civilian population or their transfer to or systematic detention in concentration or forced labour camps;
8º taking of hostages and subjecting them to 8 terrorist acts;
9º intentionally launching an attack against the 9 civilian population or their property in the knowledge that such attack will cause incidental loss of life, injury to civilians or severe damage to civilian properties and which would be excessive in relation to the military advantage anticipated;
10º perfidiously using emblems and distinctive 1 signs of humanitarian organizations or other protective signs of persons or objects recognized by International Law, with intention of killing, injuring or capturing the adversary;
11º attacking by whatever means, non- 1 defended localities or demilitarized zones which are duly recognized;
12º practices of apartheid and other inhuman 1 and degrading practices, based on racial discrimination, which give rise to outrages on human dignity;
13º transfer of the population or its part into the 1 territory occupied by a party to the conflict, its deportation within or outside the occupied territory while this is not in the interests of the population;
14º any unjustifiable delay in the repatriation of 1 war prisoners or civilian internees, after the end of active hostilities;

15º passing of sentences and carrying out of 1 executions without previous judgment pronounced by a regularly constituted court and without respecting the rights of the accused;
16º intentionally killing or injuring a person in 1 the knowledge that he/she is not party to hostilities, or when he/she was fighting but that he/she had laid down his/her arms or can no longer defend himself/herself.

Article 97: Persons protected under the
Geneva Conventions
Persons protected under the Geneva
Conventions of August 12, 1949 and their additional protocols I and II of 8 June 1977 are the following:
1° the civilian population; 1
2° civilians under the power of belligerent 2 parties;
3° the wounded, sick, shipwrecked civilians or 3 soldiers;
4° members of religious, medical and hospital 4 personnel not directly involved in hostilities;
5° war prisoners and civilian internees; 5

6° civilians and soldiers who have been 6 deprived of their liberty for reasons related to the armed conflict;
7° soldiers who are no longer able to fight for 7 whatever reason.
Civilians behaving as soldiers are not protected under this Article.

Article 6: Other acts characterised as war crimes
Article 99 of Law nº 68/2018 of 30/08/2018 determining offences and penalties in general is amended as follows:
« Any of the following acts committed in armed conflicts is a war crime:
(a) to employ poisonous weapons or other weapons calculated to cause unnecessary suffering;
(b) to attack medical units and establishments as well as to destruct and damage medical transport facilities and aircrafts;

(c) to use asphyxiating, poisonous or other similar gases, or bacteriological methods of warfare;
(d) to produce, stockpile, acquire or transport biological products or toxins, whatever their origin or method of production, for hostile purposes in armed conflict;
(e) to outrage human dignity, particularly through rape, sexual torture, forced prostitution and any form of indecent assault;
(f) to enslave and engage in slave trade, or commit slavery-like practices and forced labour in any form;
(g) to use human shields;

(h) to starve the civilian population and prevent humanitarian assistance from reaching such population;
(i) to deliberately separate children from their parents or other persons responsible for their safety and welfare;
(j) to force civilians, including children to take part in hostilities or to perform works related to military service purposes;
(k) to refuse to cater for the wounded, sick, shipwrecked persons and persons whose liberty has been restricted for reasons related to the armed conflict;
(l) to mistreat prisoners or internees;
(m) to loot or destroy public or private property;
(n) to impose collective punishment for individual acts;
(o) to commit acts of violence meant to terrorise all or part of the population. »

Article 125: Exemption from criminal liability for abortion
There is no criminal liability if abortion was performed due to the following reasons:
1º the pregnant person is a child; 1
2º the person having abortion had become 2 pregnant as a result of rape;
3º the person having abortion had become 3 pregnant after being subjected to a forced marriage;
4º the person having abortion had become 4 pregnant as a result of incest up to the second degree;
5º the pregnancy puts at risk the health of the 5 pregnant person or of the foetus.
Abortion is performed by a recognized medical doctor.
Conditions to be satisfied for a medical doctor to perform an abortion are determined by an Order of the Minister in charge of health.
If, after abortion, it is evident that the person on whom abortion was performed applied for it with no legal basis, such a person is punished as a person who performed a self-induced abortion.

Article 167: Aggravating circumstances of theft
The penalty for theft doubles if:
1° the offender committed the crime through 1 burglary, climbing or possession of keys other than the owner;
2° the theft is carried out in an occupied house 2 or in a building used for habitation or in its surroundings;

3° the theft is carried out by a civil servant 3 taking advantage of his/her duties or a person responsible for any services of general interest;
4° the offender usurps false title or insignia of 4 a civil servant or a person responsible for services of general interest or with a false mandate from public authority.
5° the theft is carried out during the night; 5
6° the theft is carried out by more than 6 one (1) person.

Article 191: Treason
Any Rwandan who:
1º uses terrorism, subversion, armed attacks 1 or threats of violence, with intention to annex Rwanda or its part to a foreign country;
2º attempts to annex the Republic of Rwanda 2 or its part under foreign domination;
3º wages a war against the Government; 3 commits an offence.
Upon conviction, he/she is liable to imprisonment for a term of not less than twenty (20) years and not more than twenty five (25) years.

Article 192: Offences classified as treason or espionage and their penalties
Any person who:
1º discloses deliberately a State secret by any 1 means with the intention to hurt the interests of the Republic of Rwanda;
2º seeks and obtains a State secret with a view 2 of disclosing it;
3º destroys or allows another person to destroy 3 anything that contains a State secret with intent to favour another country;
4
4º is entitled to have access to State secret and uses it illegally;
5
5º deliberately discloses to a person not entitled to receive a State secret that he/she obtained in the course of his/her duties or functions;
6
6º establishes, maintains or keeps relations with a foreign State, political organization, association, foreign institutions or any person working for them with a purpose of disclosing State secrets;

7º commits acts of treason with an intention of 7 undermining national defence, diplomatic relations or essential economic interest of the Rwandan Government; commits the offence of treason when the offender is a Rwandan or of espionage when the offender is a foreigner.
Upon conviction, he/she is liable, in wartime, to imprisonment for a term of not less than twenty (20) years and not more than twenty (25) years. In peacetime, he/she is liable to imprisonment for a term of not less than ten (10) years to fifteen (15) years.
If acts mentioned in Paragraph One of this
